3:Revision 2, 2026-11-16, after D34 (changes requested). What changed from revision 1: cash deposits count as trading receipts and the large ones are flagged (D31); the pass/refer/fail outcome is shown with an illustrative label (D28); the tool does not time the underwriter (D32); and the pack is written once, at sign-off. Everything else stands as revision 1 designed it, and the owner confirmed local-first (D29), OBIE v3.1 and TrueLayer first (D30) and Tier 1 only by 2026-11-29 (D33).
10:- 25-45 of a 45-60 minute review goes on 'unusual transactions, internal transfers, director loans, cash deposits'. Adding up takes 10-20.
16:- D31: cash deposits count as trading receipts, and deposits above a threshold are flagged. Revision 1 kept every cash deposit out of trading receipts until a person moved it.
21:**What the repo already does** (re-read at 43f3e57; the lines revision 1 cited are where it said). It imports a five-column CSV or a Monzo export with strict validation (src/cma/ingest.py, src/cma/importers/monzo.py). An ordered regex categoriser never guesses (src/cma/categorise.py). No rule mentions cash, so a cash deposit today falls to OTHER and shows in M16 (src/cma/categorise.py:37-50, 64-66). It computes 16 figures with evidence ids (src/cma/metrics.py:248-292); revenue is the set REVENUE_CATEGORIES (src/cma/models.py:33), which M2, M3, M4 and M9 read (src/cma/metrics.py:121, 172). It applies an illustrative YAML policy to pass, refer or fail (src/cma/policy.py:83-123, src/cma/policies/uk_sme_default.yaml), and the guard already accepts a threshold tagged [Rn] when it is introduced as a limit (src/cma/guard.py:189-201). A LangGraph pipeline drafts the memo, checks it with the guard and stops for a named reviewer (src/cma/graph.py). There is an offline template model (src/cma/llm.py:26-69) and an MCP server (src/cma/mcp_server.py).
27:| Genuine trading income separated from transfers, director money and cash | Revenue is claimed narrowly; every other credit, cash included, is one OTHER pile in M16 | No breakdown of money in; cash takings, director loans, own-account transfers and refunds look alike |
28:| Real acquirers and merchant cash advances recognised | Only synthetic acquirer names (README:125-126) | Real card revenue lands in M16 and refers the case |
29:| Large cash deposits highlighted | Nothing recognises cash | Cash is neither counted as takings nor flagged |
49:**Sub-decision: how to show what is unexplained.** (i) Redefine M16 to mean unexplained only. (ii) Keep M16's value (money in that is not trading receipts), rename it to say so, and add a figure for each bucket plus the unexplained share (M26). *Chose (ii)*: a memo already signed that cites M16 must keep its meaning, and ids are stable.
51:**Sub-decision: how cash deposits count (D31).** (i) Out of trading receipts until the underwriter moves them (revision 1). (ii) In trading receipts, with the cash inside them reported as its own figure (M18) and indicators on large deposits, cash-heavy months and cash withdrawn then paid back in. (iii) A firm setting that chooses (i) or (ii). *Chose (ii)*, the owner's answer. (i) lost because it understates a cash business such as a café and makes the underwriter add back every ordinary deposit by hand. (iii) lost because M2 would then mean different things in different packs, so a signed memo citing M2 could not be read without knowing the setting; a firm that wants a deposit out moves it with an override and a reason. What (ii) gives up: personal cash paid in by an owner lifts M2, M9 and M10 unless an indicator catches it or a person moves it (see Consequences).
57:**Sub-decision: where the categorisation rules live.** (i) Keep them as code and add more. (ii) Move the rule tables into packaged, validated data files like the policy YAML; the matching passes (reversals, round trips, pairing, profile names) stay in code. *Chose (ii)*: the lists of acquirers, lenders, cash wording and gambling operators are what changes most often, the v1 table can be kept as data for rollback, and a firm's own rules use the same format.
72:    figures layer: M1-M28, money-in buckets, monthly summary, indicators (in pence)
82:- **C1 Rule tables and categoriser** (src/cma/categorise.py; rule tables as data under src/cma/rules/). Built-in tables v1 (today's) and v2 (the new lists). v2 names cash paid in (counter, deposit machine, Post Office and PayPoint wording) as cash_deposit, and names cash-withdrawal wording that only the cash round-trip indicator reads. Precedence: per-line override, then protected built-in patterns (returned DD, director, capital, shareholder, lender, HMRC), then profile names (directors, own accounts), then firm rules, then the rest of the built-in table, then the default (a debit is supplier, a credit is unexplained). Each line records which rule set its category. A pattern that is ambiguous between buckets resolves to one that needs judgement, never to trading receipts; cash wording is not ambiguous, and counting cash as trading is the owner's decision (D31).
83:- **C2 Figures layer** (src/cma/metrics.py, new src/cma/indicators.py). Adds M17-M28, the monthly summary and the indicators, including the three cash indicators. cash_deposit is a trading-receipts category, so M2 and every figure built on it include cash, and M18 reports the cash inside M2. All sums and threshold comparisons are in integer pence. Each figure comes with its evidence and, for sums, the evidence total. A case whose buckets do not reconcile is refused.
91:- **C10 Synthetic data and evals** (src/cma/synth.py, evals/run_evals.py). New opt-in features, cash included, with truth for every bucket, line and indicator.
101:| R55 | C1 (category), C2 (M2, M18 and the figures built on them) |
131:- A firm setting that takes cash deposits out of trading receipts (see the cash sub-decision).
134:- Merchant cash advance repayments taken out of card settlements before payout; they never appear as a line.
142:Tier 1, demonstrating the mechanism (D23): R1-R7, R10-R19, R22-R31, R33-R35, R42, R43, R47, R48, R52-R56 (39 requirements), plus the stopwatch trial sheet (documentation only). It covers the v2 tables with real acquirers, lenders and cash wording, the buckets with cash inside trading receipts, the monthly summary and indicators, single-account cases with a profile and an override log, the workspace with the illustrative policy check, the preview, pack and sign-off, the offline lock, and a synthetic case shaped like a business.
149:- A cash business is not understated: takings paid in as cash count as trading, the cash inside them is its own figure, and the deposits a person should look at come first (D31).
159:- Cash paid in by the owner now counts as trading receipts. It lifts M2, M9 and M10, and can turn an illustrative fail into a pass (D31). The single-deposit, monthly-share and round-trip indicators catch the common patterns. They miss personal cash split into deposits below the limit, in months where cash stays under the share limit. The underwriter sees that only in M18 and the cash lines.
